1. DNS Configurations
  
  Record	              Purpose
  NS (Name Server)	    Lists servers that perform DNS resolution (always have more than one)
  A (Address)	          Maps a name to an IPv4 address (core DNS record); AAAA = same for IPv6
  MX (Mail Exchanger)	  Identifies the mail server for a domain
  TXT (Text)	          Stores SPF (Sender Policy Framework) / DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail) info — validates email authenticity, only needed if self-hosting email
  DMARC	                Domain-Based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance — aligns SPF/DKIM, prevents spoofing, reports abuse; policies: reject/quarantine/monitor
  
  -  TTL (Time to Live): how long a resolution is cached — shorter TTL for frequently-changing records, longer for stable ones.
  -  DNS load balancing: distributes traffic across multiple servers for redundancy.
  -  Propagation: changes can take up to 48 hours to fully propagate across the internet.
  -  Troubleshooting tools: nslookup, dig, online DNS checkers.
  -  Security: DNSSEC (prevents falsified DNS data), DoT (DNS over TLS), DoH (DNS over HTTPS) — encrypt DNS traffic.

2. DHCP Implementations
  
  Automates IP address assignment. Uses UDP port 67 (server) / UDP port 68 (client).
  
  Process — "DORA":
  
  1.  Discover – client broadcasts request
  2.  Offer – server offers an available address
  3.  Request – client requests the offered address
  4.  Acknowledge – server confirms/logs the lease
  
  Lease renewal timers:
  
  -  T1 at 50% of lease duration – client requests renewal
  -  T2 at 87.5% – retry if T1 failed
  -  If lease fully expires (e.g., device was away), a new address must be requested
  
  Reservations: map a specific IP to a device's MAC address — static assignment while still using DHCP (good for servers/printers).
  
  Scope: defines the address range, subnet mask, lease duration, default gateway; exclusions reserve certain addresses (e.g., for statically-configured devices) out of the pool.
  
  Pros: simplified management, fewer conflicts/errors, supports mobile devices. Cons: possible address exhaustion, dependency on DHCP server availability, risk of rogue DHCP servers (man-in-the-middle).
  
  Future: DHCPv6 (huge IPv6 address space), cloud-based DHCP, integration with Network Access Control (NAC) to block unauthorized devices.

3. Virtual LANs (VLANs)
  
  Logically segments devices on a switch regardless of physical location — improves security, reduces broadcast traffic, improves manageability/performance.
  
  VLAN types:
  
  -  Default VLAN – all ports start here (usually VLAN 1); can't be deleted/renamed
  -  Data VLAN – carries regular data traffic
  -  Voice VLAN – prioritized for real-time voice/video traffic
  -  Management VLAN – used to configure/monitor the switch itself (often = default VLAN, but can be changed)
  -  Native VLAN – carries untagged traffic
  
  Tagging & trunking:
  
  -  802.1Q tagging – identifies which VLAN a frame belongs to for inter-VLAN traffic
  -  Trunking – carries multiple VLANs over a single physical link (between switches, routers, firewalls)
  
  Best practices: dedicate the management VLAN to management only, minimize VLAN spanning across switches, use VLAN ACLs, audit assignments regularly, use trunking only when necessary.

4. Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)
  
  Encrypted connection over the internet providing confidentiality, integrity, and authentication.
  
  Types:
  
  -  Remote access VPN – individual admin/user to internal network
  -  Site-to-site VPN – connects two networks/locations (network-to-network)
  -  Client-to-site VPN – individual user connects to one site
  -  SSL VPN – client-to-site via browser, no client software needed
  -  IPsec VPN – strong encryption, common in corporate use
  -  MPLS VPN – label-based (not IP-based) multi-site connectivity; needs specific hardware
  
  Protocols:
  
  Protocol	  Notes
  OpenVPN	    Open-source, highly secure
  IPsec	      Encrypts at IP layer; often paired with L2TP (L2TP = tunneling/encapsulation only, no encryption; IPsec adds the encryption)
  WireGuard	  Modern, fast, strong encryption
  PPTP	      Older, less secure, but broadly compatible
  
  Risks: man-in-the-middle attacks, DNS leaks bypassing the VPN, poor configuration exposing real IP, free VPN services logging/selling data.
  
  Note: VPN only secures traffic from the connection point onward — traffic between the device and the local Wi-Fi access point is not protected by the VPN itself.

